<% const content=` <div class="card shadow-sm">
  <div class="card-body p-4">
    <h1 class="fw-bold">Developer Wiki</h1>
    <h2 class="mt-4"><strong>Application Overview</strong></h2>



    <h2 class="mt-4"><strong>Architecture</strong></h2>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col-12">
          <img src="/img/architecture.png" class="img-fluid" alt="Application Overview">
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4">The Cricket Bat Fitting application follows a structured Express MVC-style architecture. User
        interactions in the
        browser trigger Express routes, which pass control to controller logic. The controllers process fitting inputs,
        apply recommendation logic, and interact with the SQLite database for persistent storage. Complex user inputs
        such
        as the wagon wheel shot map are captured via HTML5 canvas and stored as JSON text, fulfilling the assignment
        requirement for dynamic JSON-driven content. Results are rendered back to the user through EJS templates, while
        admin features use authenticated routes secured with bcrypt password hashing.</p>

      <section class="mb-3">
        <h2><strong>Routing Structure</strong></h2>
        <p>
          The application uses a structured Express routing design to separate user fitting flows,
          administrative functions, and JSON API endpoints. Routes are grouped by purpose to maintain
          clarity, scalability, and separation of concerns.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>1. Public Fitting Routes (<code>/fit/*</code>)</h3>
        <p>
          These routes handle the multi-step bat fitting process. Each step collects user inputs and
          stores them as structured JSON for later processing and recommendations.
        </p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Route</th>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>/fit/start</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Displays the first fitting step</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/fit/step/:id</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Displays a specific fitting step</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/fit/step/:id</td>
              <td>POST</td>
              <td>Saves user input and moves to the next step</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/fit/results/:fitId</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Displays final bat recommendation and explanation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/fit/saved</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Lists previously saved fitting sessions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/fit/saved/:id/edit</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Loads a saved fitting session for editing</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h5>Key Responsibilities</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Collect structured user data</li>
          <li>Capture canvas interaction (wagon wheel) as JSON text</li>
          <li>Pass inputs to the recommendation logic</li>
          <li>Render dynamic results using EJS templates</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>2. Admin Routes (<code>/admin/*</code>)</h3>
        <p>
          Admin routes allow management of the bat database and are protected by session-based
          authentication with bcrypt password hashing.
        </p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Route</th>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/login</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Shows admin login page</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/login</td>
              <td>POST</td>
              <td>Authenticates admin using bcrypt hash comparison</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/logout</td>
              <td>POST</td>
              <td>Logs out admin and destroys session</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/bats</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Displays bat catalogue management page</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/bats/import</td>
              <td>POST</td>
              <td>Uploads CSV and performs validation + upsert</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/bats/edit/:id</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Edit bat details</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/admin/bats/edit/:id</td>
              <td>POST</td>
              <td>Update bat details in database</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h5>Security Model</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Passwords are <strong>not stored in plain text</strong></li>
          <li>Authentication uses <code>bcrypt.compare()</code> against a hashed password</li>
          <li>Successful login sets <code>req.session.isAdmin = true</code></li>
          <li>Admin routes check this session flag before allowing access</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>3. API Routes (<code>/api/*</code>)</h3>
        <p>
          API routes provide JSON responses and demonstrate server-side data exposure independent
          of rendered pages.
        </p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Route</th>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>/api/bats</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Returns a JSON list of all bats</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/api/bats/:id</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Returns details for a specific bat</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>/api/fits/:id</td>
              <td>GET</td>
              <td>Returns stored JSON fitting data</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          These routes demonstrate separation between data services and HTML rendering, fulfilling
          the assignment requirement for JSON-driven content.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>4. Route → Controller Flow</h3>
        <pre>
Client Request
   ↓
Express Route Handler
   ↓
Controller / Logic Layer
   ↓
Database + JSON Processing
   ↓
EJS View OR JSON Response
  </pre>

        <p>
          Routes are kept lightweight and primarily responsible for validating request parameters,
          calling controller functions, and returning rendered views or JSON responses.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h3>5. Error Handling</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Unknown routes return a custom 404 page</li>
          <li>Server errors are caught and logged</li>
          <li>API routes return JSON error responses while page routes render error views</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This demonstrates structured backend design beyond simple page rendering.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h2><strong>Database Schema</strong></h2>
        <p>
          The application uses SQLite as a lightweight relational database to store bat catalogue
          information and user fitting sessions. Data persistence ensures users can save and revisit
          previous fittings, fulfilling the assignment requirement for permanent artifact storage.
        </p>

        <h4>1. Tenants Table</h4>
        <p>
          The system supports a tenant structure to allow data separation. A default tenant is created
          for single-user operation, but the schema allows future expansion.
        </p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Column</th>
              <th>Type</th>
              <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>id</td>
              <td>INTEGER (PK)</td>
              <td>Unique tenant identifier</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>name</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Tenant or organisation name</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>created_at</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Timestamp of tenant creation</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h4>2. Bats Table</h4>
        <p>This table stores the cricket bat catalogue used by the recommendation engine.</p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Column</th>
              <th>Type</th>
              <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>id</td>
              <td>INTEGER (PK)</td>
              <td>Unique bat identifier</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>tenant_id</td>
              <td>INTEGER (FK)</td>
              <td>Links bat to a tenant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>brand</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Bat brand name</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>model</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Bat model name</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>size_label</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Bat size (e.g., SH, Harrow, 6)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>weight_g</td>
              <td>INTEGER</td>
              <td>Bat weight in grams</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>pickup_rating</td>
              <td>INTEGER</td>
              <td>Perceived pickup rating (1–10)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>sweet_spot</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Sweet spot position (Low, Mid, High)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>profile</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Blade profile type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>handle_shape</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Handle shape (Oval, Round)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>handle_length</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Handle length (Short, Standard, Long)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>bow</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Blade bow type (Low, Mid, High)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>notes</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Additional bat notes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>image_url</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Optional image reference</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>created_at</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Record creation timestamp</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          A unique constraint on <code>(tenant_id, brand, model, size_label)</code> ensures duplicate bats
          are not inserted during CSV imports. Instead, existing entries are updated using upsert logic.
        </p>

        <h4>3. Fits Table</h4>
        <p>
          This table stores complete user fitting sessions, including structured measurements and
          JSON-based artifacts produced during the fitting process.
        </p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Column</th>
              <th>Type</th>
              <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>id</td>
              <td>INTEGER (PK)</td>
              <td>Unique fitting session identifier</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>tenant_id</td>
              <td>INTEGER</td>
              <td>Associated tenant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>created_at</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Date and time of fitting</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>height_cm</td>
              <td>INTEGER</td>
              <td>User height measurement</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>weight_kg</td>
              <td>INTEGER</td>
              <td>User weight</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>hand_size_cm</td>
              <td>REAL</td>
              <td>Measured hand size</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>floor_to_wrist_cm</td>
              <td>REAL</td>
              <td>Reach measurement</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>shot_type</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Preferred shot style</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>conditions</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Typical playing conditions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>footwork</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Footwork style</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>batter_style</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Batting approach</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>max_distance_m</td>
              <td>INTEGER</td>
              <td>Maximum hitting distance</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>experience</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Player experience level</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>brand_filter</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Optional brand preference</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>input_json</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Serialized user fitting inputs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>shot_map_json</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Serialized canvas wagon wheel data</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>result_json</td>
              <td>TEXT</td>
              <td>Serialized recommendation and explanation</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          JSON data is stored as TEXT fields within SQLite. Controllers are responsible for
          serializing JavaScript objects into JSON strings when saving and parsing them back
          into objects when loading saved sessions.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section class="mb-5">
        <h2><strong>JSON Data Handling </strong></h2>
        <p>
          JSON is used throughout the application as the primary data interchange format between
          the front end, server logic, and database. This supports dynamic content generation and
          fulfills the requirement for JSON-driven data processing.
        </p>

        <h4>1. User Input JSON</h4>
        <p>
          During the fitting process, user selections (e.g., playing style, height, grip preference)
          are collected and structured into a JavaScript object. This object is then serialized into
          JSON and stored in the <code>input_json</code> database column.
        </p>

        <h4>2. Canvas Shot Map JSON</h4>
        <p>
          The wagon wheel canvas allows users to draw where they play shots most frequently.
          JavaScript captures click coordinates and converts them into structured JSON data.
          This JSON is stored in the <code>shot_map_json</code> column and can later be reloaded
          to recreate or analyze shot patterns.
        </p>

        <h4>3. Recommendation Result JSON</h4>
        <p>
          The recommendation engine generates structured output explaining why a particular bat
          was selected. This includes scoring factors and match reasoning, which are stored as
          JSON in the <code>result_json</code> column.
        </p>

        <h4>4. JSON Lifecycle</h4>
        <pre>
User Input / Canvas Interaction
        ↓
JavaScript Object
        ↓
JSON.stringify()
        ↓
Stored as TEXT in SQLite
        ↓
Retrieved from Database
        ↓
JSON.parse()
        ↓
Rendered in Results View or API Response
  </pre>

        <p>
          This lifecycle demonstrates how JSON acts as a flexible, structured format for capturing
          complex user-generated data while still being stored in a relational database system.
        </p>

        <h4>5. API JSON Responses</h4>
        <p>
          API routes such as <code>/api/bats</code> return database records as JSON, allowing the
          application to provide machine-readable data alongside traditional HTML views.
        </p>

        <p>
          Together, these mechanisms demonstrate full-stack JSON handling from user interaction
          to persistent storage and back to dynamic rendering.
        </p>
      </section>

<section class="mb-5">
  <h2><strong>Canvas Interaction</strong></h2>

  <p>
    The application uses an HTML5 Canvas to capture user shot-placement patterns in an interactive
    “wagon wheel” style tool. This provides advanced client-side interaction beyond standard
    form inputs and produces a structured JSON artifact that is stored alongside the fitting session.
  </p>

  <h4>1. What the User Does</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>The user clicks (or taps) on the canvas to mark common shot locations.</li>
    <li>Each interaction is recorded as a coordinate point relative to the canvas.</li>
    <li>The canvas updates immediately to provide visual feedback (points/markers/heat zones).</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>2. What the Browser Generates</h4>
  <p>
    The client-side script converts canvas interactions into a JSON structure such as an array of points
    (or zones), for example:
  </p>

  <pre>{
  "points": [
    { "x": 214, "y": 156, "type": "hit" },
    { "x": 355, "y": 240, "type": "hit" }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "canvasWidth": 600,
    "canvasHeight": 400
  }
}</pre>

  <p>
    This JSON is then serialized into a string using <code>JSON.stringify()</code> and stored in a hidden
    form field (e.g., <code>&lt;input type="hidden" name="shot_map_json"&gt;</code>). When the user submits
    the fitting step, the JSON is posted to the server as part of the normal form submission.
  </p>

  <h4>3. How the Server Stores It</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>The route handler receives the JSON string (e.g., <code>req.body.shot_map_json</code>).</li>
    <li>The controller validates the payload (non-empty JSON, expected keys, safe size limits).</li>
    <li>The JSON is stored in SQLite in the <code>fits.shot_map_json</code> TEXT column.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>4. Reloading a Saved Session</h4>
  <p>
    When a user loads a saved fit, the server retrieves <code>shot_map_json</code> from the database and
    returns it to the browser. The client script parses it using <code>JSON.parse()</code> and redraws the
    canvas markers so the user can review or edit their shot map.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-0">
    <strong>Why this matters:</strong> This demonstrates a full data lifecycle for complex user-generated
    content: Canvas interaction → JSON artifact → database persistence → later retrieval and re-rendering.
  </p>
</section>


<section class="mb-5">
  <h2><strong>Fitting Process Workflow</strong></h2>

  <p>
    The fitting process is implemented as a multi-step workflow. Each step collects a subset of user inputs,
    stores them as a fitting session, and progressively builds enough information to generate a final bat recommendation.
    The workflow supports saving sessions and reloading them for editing.
  </p>

  <h4>1. High-Level Flow (use your flowchart image here)</h4>
  <p>
    The following diagram summarises the user journey and how data moves through the system:
  </p>

  <figure class="mb-4">
    <img src="/img/fitting.png" alt="Fitting process flowchart" class="img-fluid border rounded">
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-muted">
      Fitting workflow: multi-step inputs, canvas capture, JSON persistence, recommendation generation, results display.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h4>2. Step-by-Step Behaviour</h4>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Start / New Fit:</strong> The user begins a new session and the application creates (or prepares)
      a fitting record context.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Multi-step Inputs:</strong> Each step captures structured data (e.g., measurements, preferences,
      playing style). The server stores raw inputs in <code>fits.input_json</code> and may also store key fields
      in dedicated columns (e.g., <code>height_cm</code>, <code>hand_size_cm</code>) for easier querying.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Canvas Shot Map (optional step):</strong> The user records shot locations on the canvas. This data
      is stored in <code>fits.shot_map_json</code> as a JSON artifact.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recommendation Engine:</strong> Once sufficient inputs are collected, the controller runs the matching
      logic against the bat catalogue in the <code>bats</code> table.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Result + Explanation:</strong> The selected bat and explanation factors are stored in
      <code>fits.result_json</code> and rendered on the results page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Saved Fits:</strong> Users can view prior sessions, reload them, and re-run the recommendation after edits.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h4>3. Data Artifacts Produced</h4>
  <table class="table table-bordered">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Artifact</th>
        <th>Stored In</th>
        <th>Purpose</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>input_json</code></td>
        <td><code>fits.input_json</code></td>
        <td>Full set of user selections and measurements (source of truth for the session)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>shot_map_json</code></td>
        <td><code>fits.shot_map_json</code></td>
        <td>Canvas shot placement data captured as JSON text</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>result_json</code></td>
        <td><code>fits.result_json</code></td>
        <td>Recommendation output and explanation (scoring/match reasoning)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="mb-0">
    <strong>Implementation note:</strong> JSON is stored as TEXT in SQLite. The controllers serialize objects with
    <code>JSON.stringify()</code> when saving and reconstruct them using <code>JSON.parse()</code> when loading.
  </p>
</section>



      <h5 class="mt-4">REST Endpoints</h5>
      <ul>
        <li><code>GET /api/bats</code></li>
        <li><code>POST /api/recommend</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  `;
